WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.310
- [Narrator] Orange juice
made from Florida oranges

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:04.500
might be disappearing from store shelves.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:07.110
This has sent frozen
concentrate orange juice futures

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:09.990
to record highs and led
to thousands of job cuts,

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:11.880
billions of dollars lost for the state,

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:15.273
and bigger risks that have
scared away some futures traders.

00:00:15.273 --> 00:00:16.890
(crowd shouting)

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:18.660
The impact is so far reaching

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:21.030
because orange juice isn't
just a breakfast drink,

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:23.010
it relies on a real world commodity

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:26.070
that has propped up an
entire industry for decades.

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:28.320
Here's what Florida's
dying oranges can tell us

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:30.150
about how the commodity market works

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:33.543
and how it impacts the price
you pay for orange juice.

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:40.380
A commodity is a basic
good used in commerce

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:42.660
that usually refers to a raw material used

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:44.610
to manufacture finished goods.

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:47.340
So think lumber, gold, oil,

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:49.290
and frozen concentrate orange juice,

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:52.050
which became a commodity
following World War II.

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:53.970
- Researchers were trying to find ways

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:57.420
to give soldiers in the
United States military ways

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:00.870
to drink tasty juice that
also had the added benefit

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:02.940
of giving them vitamins.

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:04.530
- [Narrator] By evaporating
some of the water

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:07.290
from orange juice then
freezing and packaging that,

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:09.810
frozen concentrate orange
juice, the commodity,

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:10.800
was invented.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:12.377
- [Announcer] Shipment
is in refrigerated trucks

00:01:12.377 --> 00:01:15.000
and trains only to keep the product frozen

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:16.493
until the can is opened in the kitchen

00:01:16.493 --> 00:01:18.660
and the water is put back in.

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:21.840
- Because it's something
that is widely processed,

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:25.680
widely available, and is
generally of equal value

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:28.680
or quality depending on where you are,

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:30.300
that's something that could be traded.

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:31.500
- [Narrator] Following this invention,

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:33.840
Florida's orange industry really boomed.

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:35.430
Throughout the late 1990s,

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:37.830
dozens of orange juice
processing plants were built

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:39.090
across the state.

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:40.230
Depending on the season,

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:43.170
more than 90% of the country's
orange juice was made

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:44.790
from Florida-grown oranges.

00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:46.200
- [David] You had Florida oranges

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:47.640
on the state's license plates.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:49.380
You have the Florida Orange Bowl,

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:51.840
which is the one of the most
famous college football games

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:53.865
played outside of Miami each year.

00:01:53.865 --> 00:01:55.740
- [TV Announcer] Miami Florida plays host

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:57.120
to a half million spectators

00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:58.980
on the eve of the Orange Bowl game.

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:01.470
- In addition, to this
actual hard commodity

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:03.870
that we have oranges
at the end of the day,

00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:06.540
you really see, in the case of Florida,

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:10.320
them growing into this
large cultural phenomenon.

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:12.240
- [Narrator] Commodity
trading often revolves

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:13.590
around futures contracts,

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:15.300
an agreement between two parties

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:17.670
for a delivery of a
commodity at a future date.

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:20.370
These tend to run on a monthly basis.

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:21.510
- [David] When prices go up,

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:24.810
that tends to mean either
there's a huge shocked supply

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:27.090
or it means there's surging demand.

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:28.110
- [Narrator] In Florida's case,

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:30.420
a bacterial disease called citrus greening

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:33.690
has been killing orange
trees since the early 2000s.

00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:36.630
That, plus a freeze and
two hurricanes last year,

00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:40.200
had led to the state's worst
harvest in nearly 90 years.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:42.660
Demand for orange juice
has actually gone down

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:43.493
over the years,

00:02:43.493 --> 00:02:45.150
but because Florida's orange production

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:46.500
has plummeted so much,

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:48.600
prices have actually gone up.

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:50.070
Orange juice manufacturers

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:53.190
are also increasingly using
oranges from Mexico and Brazil

00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:55.770
because Florida's supply
has gotten so low.

00:02:55.770 --> 00:02:56.880
- Over the last couple of years,

00:02:56.880 --> 00:02:59.130
the U.S. dollar has been
particularly strong,

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:00.300
which basically means

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:04.020
that we can have our
dollars go further abroad.

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:06.600
So imports from places
like Brazil and Mexico

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:09.090
have become effectively cheaper,

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:12.090
which adds more pressure
on Florida oranges.

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:13.440
- [Narrator] So why trade a commodity

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:15.450
like frozen concentrate orange juice?

00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:17.850
Well, for the farmer and the manufacturer,

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:19.890
buying futures contracts protects them

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:21.990
from price fluctuations in the market.

00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:24.030
- So if the price does in fact fall,

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:27.300
you've locked in some of your
output at a certain level

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:30.540
so you're not going to bear
the full brunt of those losses.

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:32.010
Commodity markets are dependent

00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:34.920
on a lot of natural
factors that are unforeseen

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:36.330
and out of people's control.

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:38.580
And one of the ways that traders try

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:41.460
to limit some of the volatility
that comes with that,

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:44.520
is by exchanging futures contracts.

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:45.780
- [Narrator] Financial traders aim

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:48.600
to profit off that
volatility by placing bets

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:51.150
on which way they think
the market will swing.

00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:54.270
That was the premise of the
1983 movie trading places

00:03:54.270 --> 00:03:56.430
where the main characters
made a fortune betting

00:03:56.430 --> 00:03:58.470
on the frozen concentrate
orange juice market.

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:00.000
- The cold winter has apparently

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:01.741
not affected the orange harvest.

00:04:01.741 --> 00:04:03.360
(crowd shouting)

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:05.220
- [Narrator] But what
happens in commodity markets

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:07.440
doesn't just affect these three groups.

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:09.090
When futures prices are high,

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:11.190
manufacturers pay more for contracts,

00:04:11.190 --> 00:04:13.110
which means they charge retailers more

00:04:13.110 --> 00:04:15.120
who then charge consumers more.

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:17.430
- Given the trends that
we're seeing from Florida,

00:04:17.430 --> 00:04:20.313
it seems that the prices will
only continue to go upward.

